In the context of programming, loops are typically used to perform an action multiple times, often with different inputs.

When writing a program, you often start with some initial conditions or a "base case" which is the first thing you want to happen when the loop runs. A common example of such a "base case" is something to do with making sure you don't get an infinite loop (that is a loop which keeps running forever: it doesn't terminate). The loop then continues from the remainder of your "base case".

One way you might define what needs to happen on your "base case" would be something like:

if your "base case" is "do nothing", then this will not happen.

The code for your loop could use something like this:

if (some condition) {
    doTheThingOnce();
}

If that "do the thing once" is also the end of the loop, then another type of loop can be used than what I've described here:
the "for" loop.

There are also "while" loops (but these are much less common) where you keep running some action until a certain condition is met - usually when your current position hits zero. 
Then the code for your loop might look like this:

while (some condition) {
    doTheThing();
}

Note that I have only described some of the most common ways loops can be used; for more information see: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loop_(computer_programming)

